 "endCard": 71.3,
 "end": {
  "title": "Next drop: where does the bounce *go*?",
  "follow": "Follow Rest Frame",
  "foot": "Huygens built the first pendulum clock in 1656. It kept time to about a minute a day."
 },
 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 2.817,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Which swing gets back first? Big? Or small?"
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 3.6,
   "dur": 9.574,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Each string is just under a meter, as in a grandfather clock. One swing: about one second. Ghosts every tenth of a second bunch up at the turns."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 13.7,
   "dur": 6.217,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Pull its twin three times farther. Let both go. Three times the distance… and still, neck and neck."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 20.8,
   "dur": 15.766,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "Galileo claimed every swing takes the same time, wide or narrow. For small swings, he was nearly right. Three times farther out, the pull is almost three times stronger. So it covers triple the path at nearly triple the speed: nearly the same time."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 38.96666666666667,
   "dur": 8.678,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Now hang fifteen, each a little shorter. Only length changes. For half a minute, they drift apart. Then, just as gradually… back in line."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 50.86666666666667,
   "dur": 9.369,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Measure it: twenty swings, and the big one is three tenths of a second behind. At thirty degrees, Galileo is off by about one part in sixty."
